{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# S5 — Bound writable container layers and alarm on exited-container debris\n\n## Why\nWritable container layers are each container's overlay2 `upperdir` inside `/var/lib/docker`.\nThey grow independently of image layers and BuildKit cache, no prune reaches them, and nothing\nmeasured them on 2026-09-02 — the only signal was \"root disk high\". Last cap under ADR 0112.\n\n## Mechanism decision, with evidence\nThe ticket offers a runtime storage quota \"when compatible\", else a reaper. **The quota is NOT\ncompatible here.** overlay2's `--storage-opt size=` (and the `overlay2.size` daemon default) is\naccepted only when the filesystem backing `/var/lib/docker` is XFS mounted with `pquota`, else\nthe daemon refuses: `--storage-opt is supported only for overlay over xfs with 'pquota' mount\noption`. That filesystem is ROOT here, and XFS reads quota options at MOUNT time and refuses\nthem on a remount — so it needs `rootflags=pquota` and a REBOOT: the constraint S4 (`2ba3`) hit\nfor `/var/tmp`, re-derived and binding here. Even after that reboot it is PER-CONTAINER, not an aggregate\nceiling, and applies at creation, so every live service must be recreated. So this ships the\nmitigation, publishes which regime the box is in, and documents the enablement steps.\n\n## What the reaper does NOT guarantee\n1. **It cannot reclaim a RUNNING container's writable layer at all.** Only exited/dead\n   containers are removable, so for the live compose set this is MEASUREMENT AND AN ALARM, not a\n   bound. Only the reboot-gated quota would bound those. Sharper than S4's fill rate, said first.\n2. For the debris it does bound, the bound is `cap + fill_rate x interval`. At the 2 GiB share\n   and a 300 s period a sustained net fill above ~7.2 MB/s outruns it; this gp3 volume does\n   125 MB/s baseline, ~17x that.\n3. Nothing that exited inside the grace window (900 s) is removed, so a burst of fresh exits is\n   unreclaimable by design. When it cannot get under the share it SAYS SO.\n\n## Safety — proved against the live set\n`docker container prune --filter until=…` cannot express what this host needs: its filters are\n`until` and `label`, with NO name filter. `autodeploy.sh` starts the mcp blue-green backends\nwith a bare `docker run` compose never sees, and reaps them itself in `mcp_retire_sweep` under a\nguard this reaper cannot replicate — it reads the nginx `/mcp/` upstream include and refuses to\nreap an exited container that is still the live backend, because `--restart always` would have\nrestored it. Reaping one anyway is bug `9ea3`: a transient exit became a permanent 502 on\n2026-09-02. So candidates are enumerated and removed individually, sparing:\n\n* anything labelled `com.docker.compose.project` (gerrit, review-bot, opcert, compose-mcp-1) —\n  an exited compose service is a crashed one whose logs are the evidence;\n* anything labelled `rebar.service` (`mcp_run_new`'s stable identity);\n* anything matching `^(rebar-mcp|compose-mcp-1)` — `mcp_managed`'s own regex, so the two agree.\n\nTwo daemon-enforced guarantees, not shell logic: candidates come from `docker ps -a --filter\nstatus=exited --filter status=dead`, which never lists a running container; removal is\n`docker rm` WITHOUT `-f`, which the daemon refuses for a running one (the precedent\n`mcp_retire_image` relies on). No `-v`/`--volumes`; `docker system prune` never used.\n\n## What invokes the reaper, and what publishes the heartbeat\nTwo DIFFERENT callers; naming only one is how a heartbeat ends up with no producer.\n* The REAPER runs from its own `rebar-container-reaper.timer` (5 min, `TimeoutStartSec` nested\n  strictly below the period per bug `1205`), rendered by `--print-units` and installed by\n  `compose-up.sh` beside the three caps it already installs. A timer rather than a hook in the\n  autodeploy tick because `prune_docker_caches` runs only on deploy/backoff/pressure paths, so a\n  quiescent `main` would never reap.\n* The HEARTBEAT is published by `observability.sh`, whose 5-minute timer already exists\n  (`install-observability.sh`); §2i calls the side-effect-free `--check-active`. So \"every tick\"\n  is the OBSERVABILITY tick, and the heartbeat has a producer even when the reaper timer is the\n  dead thing — the point of having it.\n\n## Observability (S2's publish-or-stay-silent rule)\n§2i, from the SAME single `docker system df` call §2f already makes — the format string gains\n`{{.Reclaimable}}` rather than adding a second daemon walk:\n`container_writable_bytes` (Containers Size), `container_exited_bytes` (Containers\nReclaimable), `container_writable_used_percent` (`pct_of_cap`, S3's helper, reused),\n`container_reaper_active` (1/0 heartbeat), `container_quota_enforceable` (1/0: is the kernel\nenforcing XFS project quota on the Docker root fs, i.e. could a ceiling exist at all).\nNon-heartbeat readings publish ONLY on a successful measurement.\n\n**Field surface verified — MATCH.** `docker system df --format` exposes `{{.Reclaimable}}`; on\nthe Containers row `Size` sums writable layers (`SizeRw`) and `Reclaimable` is the non-running\nsubset, i.e. the stopped-container debris. Checked against the CLI reference and Docker's\ndisk-usage docs before changing the format string. An unrecognised row or missing third field\npublishes NOTHING for that reading, so a rendering change degrades to silence, not a wrong\nnumber.\n\n## Alarms (`monitoring_autodeploy.tf`)\n`rebar-container-writable-usage-high` (>85%) and `rebar-container-reaper-not-active` (<1).\n**The second pre-empts the S4 finding**: without it a silently-dead timer leaves usage reading\nnominal while nothing enforces anything. Both `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"`, both wired to\n`aws_sns_topic.alerts`. `container_quota_enforceable` is published WITHOUT an alarm: its honest\nvalue is 0 until the reboot, and an alarm that pages continuously is muted within a day.\n\n## Which file owns what, and one budget\n`docker-storage-cap.sh` owns the BUDGET; `container-cap.sh` owns only the reaper, the checks and\nthe rendering. ADR 0112 puts this share INSIDE the Docker budget, so `docker-storage-cap.sh`\ngains a third share `DOCKER_CONTAINER_WRITABLE_BYTES` (2 GiB) carved out of the existing 20 GiB,\nleaving the image share 13 GiB; `container-cap.sh` READS it rather than re-spelling it, so there\nis no second literal to drift. 2 GiB is a starting point under ADR 0112 decision 6 and labelled\nas one: no writable-layer figure was captured at the outage, so it is sized from the split.\n\n## Scope\nIn, enumerated: NEW `infra/scripts/container-cap.sh`; `infra/scripts/docker-storage-cap.sh` (the\nthird share); `infra/scripts/observability.sh` (§2i + the extended parse);\n`infra/scripts/compose-up.sh` (installs the timer); `infra/terraform/monitoring_autodeploy.tf`;\n`infra/runbooks/review-bot-ops.md`; tests. Out: direct `/var/lib/docker/overlay2` deletion, the\nBuildKit cap (S2), journald (S3), `/var/tmp` (S4), the gate-scratch volume.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `--reap` removes an exited unowned container when the footprint exceeds the share, removes\n      nothing under it, reaps oldest-first to an 80% low-water mark, and WARNs naming the running\n      and protected bytes when it cannot get under. (`tests/scripts/test_container_cap.py`)\n- [ ] `--reap` removes nothing for each of: a `running` row; a `com.docker.compose.project`\n      label; a `rebar.service` label; a `rebar-mcp-*` / `compose-mcp-1` name; an exit inside the\n      grace window. Its argv never contains `-f`, `-v`, `--volumes`, `system prune` or\n      `container prune`. A test asserts the protected name pattern equals `mcp_managed`'s regex.\n- [ ] `--reap` removes nothing when the share cannot be read, and says so.\n- [ ] `observability.sh` publishes the three readings from a parseable Containers row and NONE\n      when it is absent or unparseable; percent clamped to 100; both heartbeats publish on every\n      tick including their 0 path; all five dimensionless.\n- [ ] `docker-storage-cap.sh --print-env` states the third share, the three shares sum to the\n      budget, and an over-subscribed split is refused.\n- [ ] Both alarms exist by name, `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"`, SNS-wired, asserted in\n      `tests/unit/test_alarm_actions_terraform.py`; `terraform fmt -check`/`validate` pass.\n- [ ] The runbook documents diagnosis, remediation, the `rootflags=pquota` + reboot steps, and\n      forbids manual overlay2 deletion.\n- [ ] Portability: Docker runtime APIs, systemd and local pytest only; no CI-provider trigger."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788590241936380001,"uuid":"4c9b843b-beca-42d2-b345-fcd26d820541"}